Dash geometry reaches the tool surface: `stroke-dash` and `stroke-gap`.

`stroke-style: "dashed"` alone is unusable below about 100px. Penpot derives
the dasharray from stroke width, not from the shape:
`calculate-dasharray` (frontend/src/app/main/ui/shapes/attrs.cljs) returns
`(or dash w+10),(or gap w+10)` for `:dashed`, so a 2px stroke dashes at 12,12
whatever it is drawn around. On a 16x16 boundary — perimeter ~64 — that is
2.7 dashes, and the form lands as four corner brackets reading as a crop
frame. This killed a design decision in D2 (lab/065): dashed enclosure for
`expert`, chosen so line style would carry meaning systematically.

The remedy is a tool argument, not a model change. Penpot 2.17.0 added
`:stroke-dash` and `:stroke-gap` to `schema:stroke-attrs`, both
`::sm/safe-number` — verified in the running backend's own
`penpot.jar:app/common/types/shape.cljc`, not from upstream source. They are
plain numbers, so `set_op`'s `:assign` routing is untouched; the stroke map
still carries `stroke-style`, which is what triggers it.

Both are omitted when None rather than sent as null: the schema is
`:closed true` and types them as numbers, so a null is a validation 500, not
a default.

Penpot reads dash/gap only on the `:dashed` branch — `:dotted` and `:mixed`
keep width-derived geometry and there is no dash-offset field in any version.
Passing dash/gap with another style is therefore refused at the tool rather
than written and silently ignored, which is the same rule the deleted SVG
fallback was removed for.

REQUIRES Penpot >= 2.17.0. Below that the fields are not in the schema and a
write carrying them is refused by `:closed true`.

